# Abdullah portfolio — living PRD and AI handoff

Last updated: 2026-10-09. This file records the user's requested changes and the current implementation state. Read it at the start of a new AI session, then follow `AGENTS.md` and the architecture documents. Keep it current as work is completed; do not treat an in-progress item as verified or finished.

## Read order and constraints

1. `.prd` — current requirements, edit ledger, evidence, and next steps.
2. `AGENTS.md` — repository rules and relevant skill/tool routing.
3. `doc/moresimple.md` — approved experience, layout, copy, and visual direction.
4. `doc/MOTION_ARCHITECTURE.md` — mandatory ownership table before animation or transition edits.
5. `doc/WORK_TO_CASES.md` — built stage geometry and checks before touching Work, Case Studies, the shared stage, or the Salam Cargo visual.

Use the `portfolio-motion` skill for hero, scroll, Work, case-study, responsive-motion, and animation-performance changes. The stack is Next.js, React, TypeScript, CSS Modules, GSAP/ScrollTrigger, and Lenis. Native WebGL is authorized for the footer liquid requirement; it does not replace GSAP or add a general animation framework. One owner must control each animated DOM property. Honor reduced motion and preserve native touch scrolling and accessible document order.

The workspace already contained unrelated uncommitted changes when these edits began. Preserve them. No dependencies or commits have been added for the changes below. Existing asset names containing `saad` are retained where renaming them would break references; the site's visible identity is Abdullah.

## Latest request: implement the optimization and mobile recommendations

On 2026-10-09 the user requested a detailed whole-site audit, then explicitly
asked to continue with implementation. The highest-impact loading, mobile,
animation-budget and navigation fixes are implemented. The approved desktop
motion, portrait identity, typefaces and three-slide case study are preserved.
No dependencies, commits or deployment were added. The original audit and the
implementation comparison are recorded in `doc/OPTIMIZATION_AUDIT.md`.

An isolated production build passed. All five routes passed 25 touch-layout
checks at 320/390/768/1024px and landscape 844px without horizontal overflow,
detected clipped text, or runtime errors. Production navigation/history/keyboard,
reduced-motion cleanup, arbitrary-scroll recovery, reading-position restoration,
no-JavaScript document order and footer lifecycle checks passed. At CPU 4× and
approximately 1.6 Mbps, two fresh-browser mobile loads measured LCP 1.556/1.576s,
versus 3.752–4.532s in the baseline. Initial subresource transfer fell from 507.7
to 387.7 KiB; JavaScript from 201.5 to 193.6 KiB; image transfer from 187.3 to
74.9 KiB. Startup long-task excess measured 211–279ms versus 780–1010ms, and the
initial layout-shift sum was 0.00030. These are local lab observations with a
warmed image server, not field Core Web Vitals or physical-phone results.

The mobile composition is immediately readable and has no ambient hero loops;
offscreen hero mutations fell from 896/second to zero. Media-specific image
sources avoid hidden desktop galleries and duplicate portrait/overview variants.
The liquid solver and desktop Lenis load on demand, with Lenis limited to home.
Touch footer economy uses a smaller field/map and 30Hz displacement updates;
missed-frame windows lower quality, then fall back to static content. The final
CPU 4× footer sample had p95 16.8ms and zero of 224 intervals over 32ms, versus
33.4ms and 58/193 in the baseline. An earlier implementation sample was 18.1ms.
Desktop handoffs/chapter samples had p95 16.8–17ms, with zero to one intervals
over 32ms per sample. A repeated full-quality desktop footer sample reached p95
33.3ms (12/209 intervals over 32ms), so adaptation was tightened to react to two
missed intervals above 25ms in a window capped at 30 frames or 500ms, then
reverified. Controlled final footer samples measured 16.9ms desktop and 16.8ms
CPU 4× touch, with no intervals over 32ms. Other sessions produced much slower
samples while sharing an animated harness page; see the audit for that variation.
The global footer now sits outside main. Launch metadata, sitemap, robots and a
social-preview image support a configured production origin.

Remaining external inputs: real contact email, GitHub/LinkedIn profiles and
production URL. An asynchronous question requested these; they have not been
supplied. `.env.example` lists the public build variables. Unknown origins omit
canonical/image URLs and sitemap entries rather than inventing a deployed host.
Physical Android/iPhone/Safari, deployed CDN behavior, hidden-tab runtime checks
and real-user INP remain unmeasured. Continue that acceptance work after launch
details are available; do not promise universal zero lag.

